Reading:
  LEVEL: the static index predicts the level strongly (gamma_a -0.1552 (-0.1913 to -0.1182)) and moves level precision from 0.0929 to 0.0914, while slope classification goes from 89 to 85 of 510. A static covariate identifies where mortality is high, not whether it is falling.
  SLOPE: gamma_b is -0.0023 (-0.0068 to 0.0018), an interval that includes zero, so the index carries no information about the rate of change and classification is unchanged at 89. Because the coefficient is effectively zero there is nothing for the covariate to assert and the residual deviation reduces to the departure-from-national contrast; its count (11) is that same quantity, not an independent result, and must not be reported as one.
  TIMEVAR: classification rises to 103, but the primary care coefficient is 0.0400 (0.0146 to 0.0648), credibly POSITIVE, i.e. more units per birth associated with higher avoidable mortality. That sign is not plausible causally and is consistent with confounding by unit size and with CNES being an establishment registry. The gain is variance absorbed by a covariate of unclear meaning, not an improvement in what the deaths establish, and is reported as such.
